      <h2 id="step-1">Step 1: Confirm where your tiny house will go</h2>
      <p>How do I buy a tiny house without regretting it? Before you look at a single listing, know where the house will legally sit. A tiny house on wheels is usually classified as an RV, so it fits RV parks, tiny home communities and land where RV living is allowed. A foundation tiny home is permitted like a house, so it needs land where zoning allows a dwelling of that size, either as a primary home or as an ADU. Many parks and communities require RV or park model certification, which rules out some homemade houses.</p>
      <p>Our posts on <a href="/blog/where-to-put-a-tiny-house/">where to put a tiny house</a> and <a href="/blog/can-i-put-a-tiny-home-in-my-backyard/">putting a tiny home in your backyard</a> cover the options, and the <a href="/blog/tiny-house-zoning-laws/">tiny house zoning laws</a> guide explains the legal detail. Rules vary; check with your local planning office.</p>

      <h2 id="step-2">Step 2: Set your budget</h2>
      <p>Build your budget from the whole project, not just the sticker price. Beyond the house, include land or lot rent, delivery, site prep, foundation or leveling, utility connections, permits, insurance and furnishing. As typical estimates, builder-built tiny homes run about $250 to $500 per sq ft depending on finish, and DIY builds about $150 to $330 per sq ft; a new tiny house trailer alone is typically $6,000 to $12,000. Our <a href="/blog/how-much-does-a-tiny-house-cost/">tiny house cost guide</a> breaks it down line by line, and <a href="/blog/are-tiny-homes-worth-it/">are tiny homes worth it?</a> looks at the trade-offs.</p>

      <h2 id="step-3">Step 3: Choose a type, and where to buy a tiny house</h2>
      <p>How can I buy a tiny house, practically speaking? There are five main routes, each with different costs and risks.</p>
      <div class="table-wrap"><table>
        <thead><tr><th>Route</th><th>Typical cost basis</th><th>Pros</th><th>Watch out for</th></tr></thead>
        <tbody>
          <tr><td>New from a builder</td><td>About $250 to $500 per sq ft</td><td>Turnkey, often certified, warranty</td><td>Wait times, deposit risk, builder stability</td></tr>
          <tr><td>Used</td><td>Varies with age, condition and certification</td><td>Lower price, available now</td><td>Hidden damage, missing paperwork, title issues</td></tr>
          <tr><td>Shell, finished by you</td><td>Between builder and DIY pricing</td><td>Structure done professionally; save on finishes</td><td>Certification may not cover your work</td></tr>
          <tr><td>Kit</td><td>Kit price plus labor and finishes</td><td>Precut parts, faster framing</td><td>What is excluded; see <a href="/blog/tiny-house-kits-vs-plans/">kits vs plans</a></td></tr>
          <tr><td>Build from plans</td><td>About $150 to $330 per sq ft DIY</td><td>Lowest cost, fully customized</td><td>Your time, skills and permits</td></tr>
        </tbody>
      </table></div>
      <p>Where to buy a tiny house depends on the route. New homes come from tiny house builders and some RV and park model dealers. Used homes are sold through tiny house listing sites, general marketplaces, and occasionally through communities or RV parks when owners move on. Shells and kits come from builders and kit companies. If you build, you need a good set of plans; our <a href="/tiny-house-plans/">tiny house plans</a> include full construction drawings, and the <a href="/blog/how-to-build-a-tiny-house/">how to build a tiny house</a> guide covers the process. Before you commit to a size, try the <a href="/tools/how-big-should-my-tiny-house-be/">size calculator</a>.</p>

      <h2 id="step-4">Step 4: Vet the builder or seller</h2>
      <p>With a builder, you are often paying a large sum months before delivery, so due diligence matters. Ask:</p>
      <ul class="check-list">
        <li>How long have you been building, and can I talk to past buyers and see a finished house in person?</li>
        <li>Is the house certified, and by whom? Will it have a VIN and title, or a certification label?</li>
        <li>What is the payment schedule, and are deposits held separately or refundable?</li>
        <li>What is included in the price: appliances, delivery, setup, taxes?</li>
        <li>What warranty do you offer, and who handles repairs after delivery?</li>
        <li>What are the trailer's GVWR and the finished weight?</li>
      </ul>
      <p>With a private seller, ask why they are selling, where the house has been parked, whether it has been moved and how often, and for any records of repairs, inspections and certification.</p>

      <div class="callout wood">
        <strong>Protect your money</strong>
        <p>Get a written contract with a delivery date, a full specification and staged payments tied to milestones. Avoid paying the full price up front, and hold back a final payment until you have inspected the finished house. For a used home, never pay before you have seen it in person and confirmed the seller holds a clear title.</p>
      </div>

      <h2 id="step-5">Step 5: Inspect the house and verify certification</h2>
      <p>This is the step that separates a sound way to purchase a tiny house from a gamble. There is no standard tiny house inspection, so hire an RV inspector or a home inspector familiar with small structures, or bring a knowledgeable builder friend. Check for:</p>
      <ul>
        <li><strong>Water damage:</strong> soft floors, stains around windows, skylights and the roof edge, and moisture under sinks and around the shower.</li>
        <li><strong>Trailer condition:</strong> rust, tire age, bearings, brakes and lights, plus the GVWR plate against the likely weight. Our <a href="/tools/tiny-house-trailer-weight-calculator/">trailer weight calculator</a> helps estimate it.</li>
        <li><strong>Systems:</strong> electrical panel and wiring, water heater, plumbing leaks, ventilation and propane fittings if present.</li>
        <li><strong>Lofts and egress:</strong> safe access, guards, and an escape window large enough to climb out of.</li>
      </ul>
      <p>Then check the paperwork. A tiny house on wheels built to RV or park model standards (NFPA 1192 or ANSI A119.5) by a builder in a certification program will usually carry a label, and is typically titled through the DMV like other trailers or RVs. A self-built house may have only a trailer title, which can make parking, financing and insurance harder. A foundation tiny home should come with permits and a final inspection or certificate of occupancy, and is bought as real property with the land. Registration and titling rules vary by state, so confirm with your DMV.</p>

      <h2 id="step-6">Step 6: Finance and insure it</h2>
      <p>Financing depends on how the house is classified. Certified tiny houses on wheels may qualify for RV loans; other common options include personal loans, builder financing and savings. A foundation tiny home on land you own may qualify for a mortgage, construction loan or home equity loan, although small homes can be harder to appraise. <a href="/blog/how-to-finance-a-tiny-home/">How to finance a tiny home</a> compares the options in detail. Line up insurance before delivery: certified homes are generally easier to insure, and lenders will usually require coverage.</p>

      <h2 id="step-7">Step 7: Plan delivery and setup</h2>
      <p>Delivery is easy to underestimate. A tiny house on wheels is heavy and tall, typically up to 13.5 ft and 8.5 ft wide, so check the route and site for low branches, tight turns, soft ground and steep driveways. You may hire a professional hauler or tow it yourself with a properly rated truck; see <a href="/blog/how-much-does-it-cost-to-move-a-tiny-home/">the cost to move a tiny home</a>. At the site, plan for leveling, blocking or a foundation, skirting, and water, sewer and power connections, each of which may need its own permit.</p>

